WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.700
FIPS 203 is the Module-Lattice-Based Key-Encapsulation Mechanism Standard.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.148
The standardized algorithm name is ML-KEM,

00:00:07.148 --> 00:00:10.360
which is associated with the Kyber family from the NIST selection process.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.490
In practical terms, ML-KEM is used for key establishment:

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:17.450
two parties use public-key operations to agree on shared secret material that can then

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.919
protect communications with symmetric cryptography.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:21.979
A key encapsulation mechanism, or KEM,

00:00:21.979 --> 00:00:26.135
is different from the older mental model of encrypting a message directly with a public

00:00:26.135 --> 00:00:26.560
key.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.064
In a KEM,

00:00:27.064 --> 00:00:31.070
one party encapsulates a shared secret using the recipient's public key and sends a

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:31.640
ciphertext.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.160
The recipient decapsulates it using the private key.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.800
Both sides then derive shared key material for protecting the session or protocol

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:38.560
exchange.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.675
ML-KEM is central because many networked systems depend on key establishment.

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:47.760
TLS, VPNs, service-to-service communication, application protocols, secure messaging,

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.160
and internal platform connections all need a way to establish session keys.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.220
If the public-key mechanism is vulnerable to future quantum attack,

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.040
long-lived sensitive traffic may be exposed to harvest-now,

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.675
decrypt-later risk depending on the data and threat model.

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:03.560
Migration will often involve hybrid deployments where classical and post-quantum

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.720
mechanisms are combined during transition.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:09.315
That helps manage compatibility and confidence while ecosystems mature.

00:01:09.315 --> 00:01:12.670
For security teams, the important questions are where key establishment happens,

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.415
which libraries and protocols are in use, which vendors control the cryptography,

00:01:16.415 --> 00:01:20.230
how certificates or keys are managed, and how interoperability will be tested.
